# Where the data lives.
#
# Leave both unset and Odysseus uses data/odysseus.db, a file on this
# machine. That is the whole development setup: no service, no account.
#
# To host it, point DATABASE_URL at a libSQL database and give it a
# token. It is the same SQLite, so the schema, the queries and the
# ingest scripts are unchanged; only these two lines move.
#
# DATABASE_URL=libsql://your-database.turso.io
# DATABASE_TOKEN=
#
# Installing the database as a Vercel integration sets its own two names
# instead, and those are read too, so nothing has to be renamed:
# TURSO_DATABASE_URL / TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN

# The scripts accept a plain file path too, for a second local database:
# ODYSSEUS_DB=data/scratch.db

# Google sign in. Without these the button does not appear at all,
# rather than appearing and failing. Register both redirect URIs in the
# Google console: one for the deployed origin, one for localhost:3100,
# each ending /auth/google/callback.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=....apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
